<BaseLayout title="Power Adders" description="Turbo, twin turbo, top-mount supercharger, centrifugal supercharger or nitrous for an LS-swapped Genesis Coupe: how each works, what it takes, and real examples from swapped Gen Coupes.">
  <h1>Power Adders</h1>
  <p>
    A healthy naturally aspirated LS makes great, reliable power in a Genesis Coupe. Most N/A swaps on our
    <a href="/hp-wars">HP Wars</a> board land between 300 and 440 wheel horsepower. If you want more than that, the biggest gains come from
    forcing more air into the engine with a <strong>turbo</strong> or <strong>supercharger</strong>, or adding oxygen with <strong>nitrous</strong>.
  </p>

  <GuideTabs />

  <div class="tab-panel" id="overview">
  <h2 id="why">Why Add Boost or Nitrous?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Big power per dollar.</strong> A power adder can add 150–500+ hp to an engine that's already in the car, where a bigger N/A build usually means a new engine.</li>
    <li><strong>Pick the power curve you want.</strong> A top-mount blower hits hard everywhere, a centrifugal pulls hardest up top like a big N/A motor, turbos can make huge power once spooled, and nitrous only works when you press the button.</li>
    <li><strong>It suits the LS.</strong> Even stock-bottom-end LS engines take moderate boost or a modest nitrous shot well with a good tune. Forged internals open the door to four-figure numbers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="plan">Plan for the Whole Car</h2>
  <p>More power stresses everything behind and around the engine. Budget for these before you buy the kit:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fuel system:</strong> bigger injectors, a pump that holds pressure at full demand, and a boost-referenced regulator for boost. See <a href="/guides/fuel-system">Fuel System</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Engine management and tuning:</strong> a MAP sensor that reads your boost (a 1-bar won't), conservative timing and a good wideband. See <a href="/guides/ecu">ECUs &amp; Tuning</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Cooling and heat:</strong> the Gen Coupe engine bay is tight and hot already. Intercoolers, heat exchangers and turbo plumbing all compete with the radiator for space. See <a href="/guides/cooling">Cooling</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Driveline:</strong> stock Genesis axles and the factory diff are the usual first failures. See <a href="/guides/differential">Differential &amp; Axles</a> and <a href="/guides/transmission">Transmission &amp; Clutch</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Oiling:</strong> sustained high rpm and boost are hard on a small oil pan. See the <a href="/guides/swap-kits#oil-pan">Oil Pan</a> notes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="compare">Quick Comparison</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Power delivery</th><th>Biggest challenge in a Gen Coupe</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="#single-turbo">Single turbo</a></td><td>Builds with rpm and spool; huge top end</td><td>Custom hot side, heat in the bay</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#twin-turbo">Twin turbo</a></td><td>Quicker spool than one big turbo</td><td>Fitting two turbos and all the plumbing</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#top-mount">Top-mount supercharger</a></td><td>Instant boost and torque from idle</td><td>Hood clearance and intercooler heat exchanger</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#centrifugal">Centrifugal supercharger</a></td><td>Boost climbs with rpm, strongest up top</td><td>Bracket, belt and intercooler routing</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#nitrous">Nitrous</a></td><td>On demand, only when armed and triggered</td><td>Fueling and safeties; bottle refills</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <div class="tab-panel" id="single-turbo">
  <h2 id="single-turbo-how">Single Turbo</h2>
  <p>
    A turbo uses exhaust energy to spin a turbine, which drives a compressor that pushes air into the engine. One larger turbo is
    the simplest way to make very big power: one turbo, one wastegate, one blow-off valve, and an intercooler.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Power:</strong> scales with turbo size and boost. Big singles make the most peak power, but take longer to spool.</li>
    <li><strong>What it takes:</strong> custom hot side (turbo manifolds and crossover pipe), downpipe, intercooler and charge piping, oil feed and drain, wastegate, blow-off valve, and boost control.</li>
    <li><strong>Gen Coupe fit:</strong> there's no bolt-on kit, so plan for custom fabrication and a lot of heat shielding near the brake booster, wiring and fuse box.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="single-turbo-examples">What it looks like in a Gen Coupe</h3>
  <p>
    The common LS layout puts the turbo low in a front corner of the bay, ahead of the cylinder head, with the hot side wrapped in heat shielding.
    Charge pipes run out to a large front-mount intercooler in the bumper and back up to the throttle body.
  </p>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="single-turbo-ls-bay.jpg" alt="Single turbo LS in a Genesis Coupe with the turbo low in the front corner, wrapped hot side and front-mount intercooler" caption="Turbo low in the front corner, wrapped hot side, front-mount intercooler." />
    <Figure src="single-turbo-ls-flag-intake.jpg" alt="Single turbo LS with a flag-painted intake manifold in a teal Genesis Coupe, turbo in the front corner with the downpipe running up past the strut tower" caption="Big single in the front corner, downpipe running up past the strut tower." />
    <Figure src="single-turbo-ls-teal.jpg" alt="Teal BK2 Genesis Coupe with a single turbo LS visible under the open hood and a front-mount intercooler behind the grille" caption="The finished look: big single turbo under the hood, intercooler behind the grille." />
    <Figure src="single-turbo-ls-btr.jpg" alt="Single turbo LS with a BTR intake manifold in a Genesis Coupe, wrapped turbo in the driver side front corner" caption="Single turbo LS with a BTR intake: wrapped turbo in the front corner, charge pipe across the front to the throttle body." />
  </div>
  <h3 id="villegas-single">Example: Armando Villegas's Center-Mount Single Turbo</h3>
  <p>
    A different approach: Armando mounted a big Garrett turbo front and center, ahead of the engine, with both banks' hot side merging into it.
    It feeds an air-to-water intercooler (with its own Remco pump), and the radiators are split to each side of the front end.
  </p>
  <div class="fig-small">
    <Figure src="villegas-single-turbo.jpg" alt="Armando Villegas's Genesis Coupe with a large Garrett single turbo center-mounted ahead of the LS engine, an air-to-water intercooler and split CSF radiators" caption="Armando Villegas: big Garrett turbo center-mounted up front, air-to-water intercooler, split CSF radiators." />
  </div>
  <p>Single-turbo builds on our boards include Harold Calveiro's Pulsar G40 2JZ on <a href="/time-slips">Time Slips</a>. Have an LS single turbo build? <a href="/contact">Send photos</a> and we'll feature it here.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tab-panel" id="twin-turbo">
  <h2 id="twin-turbo-how">Twin Turbo</h2>
  <p>
    Two turbos, usually one per cylinder bank. Each turbo can be smaller than a single that makes the same power, so they spool
    sooner, and each bank gets its own short hot side.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Power:</strong> the top of what's been done in these cars. Anthony Pared's forged 5.3 makes 1,224 whp on twin BorgWarner S366s on <a href="/hp-wars">HP Wars</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>What it takes:</strong> everything a single needs, times two: two wastegates (or internally gated turbos), two oil feeds and drains, and charge piping that merges into one intercooler.</li>
    <li><strong>Gen Coupe fit:</strong> the turbos usually sit forward of the heads, near the frame rails, so radiator and intercooler packaging is the hard part.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="kapeluch-twins">Example: Robert Kapeluch's Twin Turbo 6.0</h3>
  <p>
    Iron-block 6.0 with a custom twin Boost Lab 6766 kit at 23 psi on flex fuel, roughly 1,200 hp, through an FTI Powerglide and a
    <a href="/guides/differential#kapeluch-88">Ford 8.8</a>. It has run <a href="/time-slips#quarter">8.867 @ 156.99 mph</a> in the quarter.
  </p>
  <div class="fig-small">
    <Figure src="kapeluch-twin-turbo-bay.jpg" alt="Twin turbo 6.0 LS in a Genesis Coupe with turbos mounted forward of the heads and a front-mount intercooler" caption="Robert Kapeluch's twin turbo 6.0: turbos sit forward of the heads, feeding a front-mount intercooler." />
  </div>
  <h3 id="shoulta-twins">Example: Dustin Shoulta's Twin Turbo</h3>
  <p>
    Dustin's setup puts one turbo on each side of the engine bay, up in the headlight area, with the downpipes running up and back past the strut towers.
    The cold side merges into a single pipe that drops down to a large front-mount intercooler, then back up to the throttle body.
  </p>
  <div class="fig-small">
    <Figure src="shoulta-twin-turbo.jpg" alt="Dustin Shoulta's twin turbo LS Genesis Coupe with a turbo on each side of the engine bay and a large front-mount intercooler" caption="Dustin Shoulta's twin turbo: one turbo per side in the headlight area, feeding a big front-mount intercooler." />
  </div>
  <h3 id="twin-turbo-more">More twin turbo layouts</h3>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="twin-turbo-ls-blue.jpg" alt="Twin turbo LS in a blue BK2 Genesis Coupe with wrapped hot side, Turbosmart wastegates and blow-off valves" caption="Twin turbo LS in a BK2 with wrapped hot side and Turbosmart wastegates and blow-off valves." />
    <Figure src="twin-turbo-ls-green-bay.jpg" alt="Twin turbo LS in a black Genesis Coupe with a green painted engine bay, turbos mounted in the front corners and a Y-pipe merging into the throttle body" caption="Turbos low in each front corner, with the charge pipes merging in a Y into the throttle body." />
  </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tab-panel" id="top-mount">
  <h2 id="top-mount-how">Top-Mount Supercharger</h2>
  <p>
    A positive-displacement blower (Roots or twin-screw) sits on top of the engine in place of the intake manifold and is belt-driven
    from the crank. It moves a fixed amount of air every revolution, so boost comes in right off idle and stays roughly flat across the rev range.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Power:</strong> instant torque everywhere, which makes for a great street car. Factory LSA and LS9 engines come this way.</li>
    <li><strong>What it takes:</strong> the blower and drive, an intercooler brick inside the blower with a front-mount heat exchanger, a coolant pump and reservoir, and a smaller pulley to raise boost.</li>
    <li><strong>Gen Coupe fit:</strong> check hood clearance first. A top-mount sits much taller than a stock intake, and the heat exchanger needs space in front of the radiator.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Examples on our boards: Aaron's LY6 with an LSA blower makes 517 whp on <a href="/hp-wars">HP Wars</a>, and Matt Haugen's
    Whipple-supercharged 427 LS3 made around 1,000 whp (see <a href="/guides/engine-selection#builds">Engine Selection</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="magnuson-top-mount.jpg" alt="Magnuson top-mount supercharger on an LS engine in a Genesis Coupe with a carbon fiber inlet tube" caption="Magnuson top-mount supercharger on an LS in a Gen Coupe." />
    <Figure src="lsa-top-mount.jpg" alt="Lime green painted LSA supercharger lid and matching inlet tube on an LS engine in a Genesis Coupe" caption="LSA supercharger with a painted lid and matching inlet tube." />
  </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tab-panel" id="centrifugal">
  <h2 id="centrifugal-how">Centrifugal Supercharger</h2>
  <p>
    A centrifugal blower looks and works like the compressor half of a turbo, but it's driven by a belt from the crank instead of by exhaust.
    It mounts on a bracket at the front of the engine and feeds the throttle body through an intercooler.
  </p>
  <h3 id="centrifugal-boost">How the boost works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Boost rises with rpm.</strong> The impeller's output climbs steeply with its speed, so boost is low down low and builds all the way to redline. The car drives like a big naturally aspirated engine and pulls hardest at the top.</li>
    <li><strong>The pulleys set the boost.</strong> A bigger crank pulley or a smaller blower pulley spins the impeller faster and makes more boost.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch impeller speed.</strong> Each blower has a maximum impeller rpm. Impeller speed = engine rpm × (crank pulley ÷ blower pulley) × the blower's internal step-up ratio. On a Vortech V-2 with a 8.35" crank pulley, a 3.5" blower pulley and a 3.61:1 step-up, 6,800 rpm works out to about 58,600 impeller rpm.</li>
    <li><strong>Supporting parts:</strong> a blow-off valve, a boost-referenced fuel pressure regulator, a MAP sensor that reads your peak boost, and an intercooler or water/methanol injection to control intake air temperature.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="centrifugal-examples">Examples</h3>
  <p>
    Alexander Knight's 427 LS3 with a <strong>Vortech V2-Ti</strong> at about 11 psi makes 711.61 whp (see the <a href="/featured/alexander-knight-2010-bk1">featured build</a>),
    and Adrian Rodriguez's 4.8 runs a <strong>TorqueStorm</strong> at roughly 500 hp (see his <a href="/featured/adrian-rodriguez-torquestorm-48">featured build</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="alex-bk1-engine-bay.jpg" alt="Vortech V2-Ti centrifugal supercharger on a 427 LS3 in a red 2010 Genesis Coupe" caption="Alexander's Vortech V2-Ti on the 427 LS3." />
    <Figure src="adrian-gc-engine-bay.jpg" alt="TorqueStorm centrifugal supercharger on a 4.8 LS in a Genesis Coupe with a front-mount intercooler" caption="Adrian's TorqueStorm on the 4.8, with a front-mount intercooler." />
    <Figure src="centrifugal-ls-driver-side.jpg" alt="Centrifugal supercharger mounted low on the driver side of an LS engine in a Genesis Coupe with a front-mount intercooler" caption="Centrifugal blower mounted low on the driver side, feeding a front-mount intercooler." />
  </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tab-panel" id="nitrous">
  <h2 id="nitrous-how">Nitrous</h2>
  <p>
    Nitrous oxide adds extra oxygen to the intake charge so the engine can burn more fuel. It only works when the system is armed and the
    activation conditions are met, so the car drives like a normal N/A car the rest of the time. A <strong>wet</strong> kit adds its own fuel
    with the nitrous; a <strong>dry</strong> kit adds only nitrous and lets the ECU add the fuel through the injectors.
  </p>

  <h3 id="kotoun-nitrous">Eric Kotoun's Holley-Controlled Progressive Dry Nitrous</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>2013 Genesis Coupe · 6.0 LS (LY6) · 200 hp dry shot · Holley Terminator X.</strong>
    Instead of a standalone nitrous controller, Eric's Terminator X controls activation, the progressive ramp, the extra fuel, the timing and the safeties.
    His car is on <a href="/hp-wars">HP Wars</a> at 395 whp N/A and 642.1 whp on an earlier 150 shot.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hardware:</strong> Nitrous Outlet dry plate, nitrous solenoid, purge, bottle heater, and dedicated relays and wiring.</li>
    <li><strong>Cabin controls:</strong> arm switch, purge button and bottle heater switch, mounted in the factory center console.</li>
    <li><strong>Arm switch:</strong> turns on the system and his nitrous-specific fuel and timing calibration. Off, the car runs its normal N/A tune. Armed does not mean spraying: the ECU still waits for its activation conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Activation:</strong> throttle above 95%, above 3,500 rpm, shutoff around 7,000 rpm, clutch switch cuts nitrous during shifts, and air/fuel ratio safety limits.</li>
    <li><strong>Progressive ramp:</strong> a PWM solid-state relay ramps the solenoid from about 15% to 100% over 3 seconds to soften the hit on the drivetrain. After a shift it restarts at about 60% instead of the full shot.</li>
    <li><strong>Holley output:</strong> a ground-switched Holley output commands the relay, so the solenoid's current never runs through the ECU. A standard mechanical relay isn't built for PWM switching; use a solid-state relay rated for the solenoid's current, switching frequency and inductive load.</li>
    <li><strong>Fuel and timing:</strong> the extra fuel comes through the injectors, so injector size, pump flow and stable fuel pressure are critical. About 8° of timing retard for the 200 shot.</li>
    <li><strong>Bottle pressure:</strong> targets about 950 psi. Pressure drops as the bottle cools, which changes how much nitrous flows. Use a pressure-based heater controller, never heat past the bottle's rated limits, and fuse and relay the heater circuit properly.</li>
    <li><strong>Purge:</strong> a momentary button clears vapor from the feed line so liquid nitrous is at the solenoid when the pass starts.</li>
    <li><strong>Wiring:</strong> size each circuit for its actual current and length, fuse close to the source, use solid grounds, and keep wiring away from heat and sharp edges.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Safety:</strong> AFR limits alone can't guarantee safe operation, especially if fuel pressure drops or a sensor fails. Eric recommends a fuel-pressure safety strategy as well, and checking every fuel and timing change against the actual tune.</p>
  </div>
